#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/7e6f6662d9597683762bcc48164e5d1227ae6a1a6bdea4243a3a3f4ca314e10a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/2bad707ee54e56c4171b99b13a36135522ab83069ab3b0291f3254850502226d.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172da751-b7b1-5993-92ce-0b3a89fc22b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305034} {:answer/id #uuid "cba6ca18-28a9-5486-94cd-df83dc6c91fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305035} {:answer/id #uuid "c5612fe7-40d4-514a-8685-6d64f1a2fd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305036} {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2bbd98-032e-589e-acc1-f778f65eceac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305037} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc281f46-91de-5d1b-9764-36cdc503c98f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78edb6bd-ed48-54d3-90d2-5d11cd8b4ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126053" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "def0abb8-e7f1-5004-ae17-5155299788f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379415} {:answer/id #uuid "42455bf1-f82b-5934-a543-60915b3f6f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379416} {:answer/id #uuid "38e359bd-12c7-5e88-9aec-6cd31e050c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379417} {:answer/id #uuid "57c27147-63ce-52f6-b6df-a82497bd3f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379418} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbfc6e2-fd3f-5436-aae4-e645605e75f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbfc6e2-fd3f-5436-aae4-e645605e75f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379419} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac58a6b3-2ab2-5915-a9c6-1cbb3af9c684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9a5bb55-99d4-5b06-9d25-2f1d72810107" :question/math-academy-id "q-126057" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number obtained when throwing a die\n- The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl\n- The speed limit on a certain road" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The speed limit on a certain road is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d22ce36-5e84-5c66-be47-bed6dc2e8038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379422} {:answer/id #uuid "77d664fc-4b1f-5ad8-b088-cc5c61b853b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379423} {:answer/id #uuid "7e09985e-4f63-5ae9-b228-9a965efd5fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379424} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8f4e06-e39b-577f-a7cb-bd4906134ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379425} {:answer/id #uuid "d86a7863-483c-5723-b40b-5e1239a2335c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d664fc-4b1f-5ad8-b088-cc5c61b853b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379423} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ddb998-5102-5008-9d6a-be0f6cb99023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695d2423-9f0c-53a2-910e-cf416ebb67bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-126061" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals\n- The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$\n- The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80dc96e1-9b3b-56f5-9be5-f1015de6758f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102122} {:answer/id #uuid "861508e3-a155-5e68-8331-5cfd882b6403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102123} {:answer/id #uuid "02de24d2-4300-564e-98d6-298bcd5eb95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102124} {:answer/id #uuid "52b1c447-48e0-5501-bc8f-8c1049104d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102125} {:answer/id #uuid "604d4c7c-f9aa-5af2-9d40-738e08be00b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "861508e3-a155-5e68-8331-5cfd882b6403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102123} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9df4213-3d40-5d1a-b3cc-9c6f34432b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131f397c-26fd-57a9-a7d9-cabc7db579b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158315" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{3}]-[{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd6f9ba-8bc4-574d-afaa-04d16a680351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379429} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfad427-41a5-52a4-90ae-fb95e9a0ab4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379430} {:answer/id #uuid "697aaea7-cc8f-5a21-897d-be261bed424b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379431} {:answer/id #uuid "10b60963-0aa1-51fd-8fd1-c3f7ab235311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379432} {:answer/id #uuid "f107b48e-c3b5-5fdc-8161-ef98f2013fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd6f9ba-8bc4-574d-afaa-04d16a680351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379429} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a54673-44eb-597a-b477-2c8c96daec96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2748b840-0359-55b1-82e2-d468720777e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158414" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} {:answer/id #uuid "a089c314-9a63-5f70-9807-f86b76748344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305042} {:answer/id #uuid "57a88cc7-240e-5e66-af6b-5db9a3880227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305043} {:answer/id #uuid "771dcc0d-7986-5c88-8d66-a61d9bd57629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305044} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae8ded-5727-59c9-9a75-04864edb3a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09fc0563-1988-57f7-bb4c-7d963fcceb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5416699-4477-5a8c-886f-c7fd7a060bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c694066-1106-59d2-ad9b-3f01367d7aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef27a7d-b7e2-5792-8e6b-1484643b34e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379436} {:answer/id #uuid "60b90ed2-8efe-55b4-ad9a-2a13c8c4b93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379437} {:answer/id #uuid "f2324ff1-7596-5acf-836a-21ef3e5ec702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379438} {:answer/id #uuid "25a56bd7-9eb2-519a-806d-81c81efea590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379439} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee397ee-0d18-50ec-989e-bc21a4b79de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a56bd7-9eb2-519a-806d-81c81efea590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379439} :answer-field/id #uuid "de60368d-ae7e-5f26-9f09-787d4665dc4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b0485b-974e-561b-8f7e-8240d4d2ce2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158425" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/87c5e1b3307b2a42fff6a97408c0475dbe242bef4a9dbfbabcec407b3fa23ce1.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51366a49-fd12-5786-8f7d-f07bc638c401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305055} {:answer/id #uuid "997c191b-7355-5162-8345-97f79fb7d478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056} {:answer/id #uuid "437df922-4149-5cc6-94d7-2f7829f9639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057} {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} {:answer/id #uuid "5718d1cc-559e-5b30-ac50-6efe49b4568a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40f5ebcd-03b2-513b-839f-8c5b12075845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} :answer-field/id #uuid "84690231-57c0-5dab-af90-fa45238c5de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3503a51d-9484-5e3d-a810-963c28370c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87813731-26fb-5d8f-9093-9813c6cb87e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102128} {:answer/id #uuid "0fae9956-6d1e-5da1-8d00-0313ee54ebcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102129} {:answer/id #uuid "19e34c6d-9f9b-5fd8-aebf-665e75533e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102130} {:answer/id #uuid "4882407f-cfa7-5648-9d25-8616a3967f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102131} {:answer/id #uuid "72c9eaca-0ecf-5bcf-ad4f-5aab57e5a25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19e34c6d-9f9b-5fd8-aebf-665e75533e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102130} :answer-field/id #uuid "d218f0c8-327d-5908-8c75-bae430386803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3535bc96-e0a2-5dcb-9a5f-4a6bf759302b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158437" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d50ec8-8818-522f-b6f5-05673b9784a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379443} {:answer/id #uuid "d958f4d8-57fc-5383-b68b-63f16b3d7a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379444} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0a6412-5b49-5359-9aa3-cf5158ba3049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379445} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa53ef2-7cc0-52e9-8f0d-8bd9c145c277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379446} {:answer/id #uuid "eb26d6e3-1943-5ed5-849c-7b1854036c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d958f4d8-57fc-5383-b68b-63f16b3d7a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379444} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd31ced5-5de1-583c-abfa-e86a8372459a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd8f04ea-bc7c-5600-a918-9219fb398cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158444" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{k},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2k}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{2k}-\\frac{9}{2k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2eaee4-b5bc-5e8b-810c-e8d157ffd3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3b9c66-3a41-5161-80eb-07798c4cd034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0eb847-8d62-591e-8b45-6ab03a14bc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305092} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a7ba7-ed75-5374-aa83-b7ecf6165e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d062a76-0166-5376-b966-5c2b21f76c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a332853-7bb0-59d4-a686-56e556a86931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87d23167-a7f7-5483-8432-8ac06af4b428" :question/math-academy-id "q-158447" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8d419b6-7d89-59ee-bdf1-076e957cb3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379450} {:answer/id #uuid "426e6217-15ce-5856-9e89-eaf6e463a4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379451} {:answer/id #uuid "f83de66a-c179-5fbc-8759-e545544ed278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379452} {:answer/id #uuid "c151d2b4-2690-59b3-bf65-903bf7f5e355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379453} {:answer/id #uuid "1723237a-d492-59e5-a297-4133998b7839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d419b6-7d89-59ee-bdf1-076e957cb3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ac95d4-9d42-53f1-abed-a9d99e0b0fa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3422e9c0-2baf-5d35-9d4c-d22907f92c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86915" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}+\\frac{k}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ceb8dbd-22a8-550d-bec9-b403a6c6db0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379457} {:answer/id #uuid "bc427281-a345-53a1-9f9b-180a933edc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379458} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9ed8a4-cc99-5c5d-81aa-737f4ef70094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379459} {:answer/id #uuid "fc262b1f-909d-57f6-9076-3a0118ebbfd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379460} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb9eaa4-a7f6-5866-9bc6-66414d9e01f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ceb8dbd-22a8-550d-bec9-b403a6c6db0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1df145-c257-5e2c-b637-a7ba4dff5e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9320a099-8469-5e83-8243-caaa61ac2eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87151" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped twice\n- The height of a randomly selected person, measured in meters\n- The concentration, in grams per cubic centimeter, of glucose in a person's bloodstream" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b55a9456-3c67-5cf2-9424-18a7c9b965a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105258} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f42651-4298-57cd-8a38-60a1768a090a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105259} {:answer/id #uuid "f1999e7a-797f-5984-9254-c6dc7812ace2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105260} {:answer/id #uuid "03d7d03f-526b-5ae3-868a-d721e0593814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105261} {:answer/id #uuid "0de5ce56-0722-59cb-8ff0-c5c3f711b467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1999e7a-797f-5984-9254-c6dc7812ace2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105260} :answer-field/id #uuid "963861f0-9b2e-52dd-9b96-c2d3fdcc68f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b249c3f7-b17f-5e21-a581-a52a50cd8b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-87154" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The weight, in kilograms, of sugar in a randomly selected bag with a capacity of $1\\,\\text{kg}$\n- The number of students in a randomly selected classroom\n- The outcome of flipping a coin" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nSome common examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,1].$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are heads or tails, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91de6dfc-3550-5227-826f-617909d90dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105348} {:answer/id #uuid "becf5a88-f447-5bbc-9a00-579a74ecfe2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105349} {:answer/id #uuid "e6038154-3664-5600-a7d9-77e6082263e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105350} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} {:answer/id #uuid "450ea2f9-252f-5675-b796-03caa076d7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3d8b68-2035-520d-82f9-f67ea75460d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57ead9aa-937c-57a1-8fa5-6d07fce39ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98912" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}]}
